{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "281",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 30s (phototype 3, occupational sun exposure, fewer than 50 nevi) with no relevant medical history presented with a left cervical lesion. A few months after initial treatment, a lymph node metastasis with extracapsular extension was detected. Five months later, another locoregional recurrence with skin metastasis was observed.\n\nPatient had fewer than 50 nevi on examination.\n\n{'Initial Staging': '- CT scan, ultrasonography, lactate dehydrogenase and S-100 protein levels revealed nothing abnormal\\n- Selective sentinel node biopsy findings were negative', 'Histopathology': '- Initial lesion: Breslow depth 2.6 mm, no ulceration, 7 mitoses/mm2\\n- BRAF V600E mutation positive', 'Imaging Studies': {'Title': 'Dermoscopic and Histopathological Images', 'Description': '- Figure A & B: Dermoscopic evolution of preexisting pigmented lesion showing involutive characteristics (5 month interval, 7mm diameter)\\n- Figure C & D: Compound nevus with abundant melanophages and BRAF V600E immunohistochemical staining\\n- Figure E & F: Dermoscopic changes in preexisting pigmented lesion showing broadened network, darkening and eccentric growth\\n- Figure G & H: Histopathological study showing infiltrating melanoma, negative for BRAF V600E mutation'}}",
